{"authored" [{"question" "q-31213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "443aa51b1667cba3cc704ff1154a3f93131e37231a0a56b6b9c9eb3e1b3e79f3" "basis" 1303 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "443aa51b1667cba3cc704ff1154a3f93131e37231a0a56b6b9c9eb3e1b3e79f3" "basis" 1303 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/C