a_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "443aa51b1667cba3cc704ff1154a3f93131e37231a0a56b6b9c9eb3e1b3e79f3" "basis" 1303 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "443aa51b1667cba3cc704ff1154a3f93131e37231a0a56b6b9c9eb3e1b3e79f3" "basis" 1303 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ and $x=4$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "443aa51b1667cba3cc704ff1154a3f93131e37231a0a56b6b9c9eb3e1b3e79f3" "basis" 1303 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ and $x=4$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/state.json" "file_sha256" "443aa51b1667cba3cc704ff1154a3f93131e37231a0a56b6b9c9eb3e1b3e79f3" "basis" 1303 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814c19e51d37a9c9f69a55428cd1cb7bf2793818f57cd8d02c50fdbd1af6807e"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-4"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=4"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-2464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=12x-7+\\frac{3}{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814c19e51d37a9c9f69a55428cd1cb7bf2793818f57cd8d02c50fdbd1af6807e"} {"question" "q-21035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-4"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=3"] ["math" "x=4"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "There are no points of inflection"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=3x-7+\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(1-\\frac{3}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=3.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=2$ and $x=4$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2}{(2)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(2)^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(4) & =\\frac{2}{(4)^{3}}-\\frac{6}{(4)^{4}}=\\frac{1}{128}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814c19e51d37a9c9f69a55428cd1cb7bf2793818f57cd8d02c50fdbd1af6807e"} {"question" "q-21041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-2"] ["math" "x=-3"] ["math" "x=2"] ["math" "x=3"] ["text" "There are no points of inflection"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-21041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/assets/19e9763d62ca7818024bf49522a127a48dd04f76d1175c93e060a1043b6a5795.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=0$ and $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "814c19e51d37a9c9f69a55428cd1cb7bf2793818f57cd8d02c50fdbd1af6807e"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=0"] ["text" "$x=-4$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=-4,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$"] ["text" "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=4$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-31213" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=0$ and $x=4$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-79198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/assets/d2a8410c92775ef68678a1cac187b3ebc717cfa9fa737ce9723c531616706717.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040224/state.json" "file_sha256" "73412ab3d2221a25a71ea63612f2620feb9ab6d007f37ee4532e683025fddd8c"} {"question" "q-79198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph 